## Callers map (28a, verified 2026-08-11)

PREMISE REFUTED: Response.usage does NOT use Langertha::Usage. Response.usage
is a plain Maybe[HashRef] holding raw provider data. The Usage/Pricing/Cost/
UsageRecord value objects are a SEPARATE, currently-unwired lane.

| Class                  | Callers in lib/            | Callers in t/                       |
|------------------------|----------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| Langertha::Usage       | Metrics.pm only            | 90_value_objects.t, (98_metrics.t)  |
| Langertha::Pricing     | Metrics.pm only            | 90_value_objects.t, (98_metrics.t)  |
| Langertha::Cost        | Metrics.pm, Pricing.pm     | 90_value_objects.t, (98_metrics.t)  |
| Langertha::UsageRecord | Metrics.pm only            | 90_value_objects.t, (98_metrics.t)  |
| Langertha::Metrics     | ZERO                       | 98_metrics.t, 90_value_objects.t, 00_load.t |

Note: the `$raider->metrics` hits in Raider.pm / t/82 / t/83 are an unrelated
plain HashRef attribute on Raider (raids/iterations/tool_calls/time_ms) — NOT
Langertha::Metrics. The "zero internal callers" claim for the module is
CONFIRMED.

Implication: no engine, role, or Response code path constructs a Usage, Cost
or UsageRecord. The whole value-object lane exists for external consumers
(Skeid/Knarr) and for Metrics.pm to delegate to. Attribute-shape changes are
therefore internally free but externally breaking — back-compat risk lives
outside this dist.

### Per-engine raw Response.usage shapes (28d input, not a full audit)

No engine normalizes; every one passes provider data through verbatim:
- Role::OpenAICompatible  -> prompt_tokens / completion_tokens / total_tokens
- Engine::AnthropicBase   -> input_tokens / output_tokens (+ cache_creation_input_tokens, cache_read_input_tokens)
- Engine::Gemini          -> raw `usageMetadata` (camelCase!)
- Engine::Ollama          -> prompt_eval_count / eval_count
- Engine::OpenAIResponses -> pre-normalized hash (only engine that touches it)
- Engine::LMStudio        -> passthrough

Cross-provider consistency exists ONLY via the Response accessors
prompt_tokens / completion_tokens / total_tokens. 28d should be re-scoped:
the invariant to audit is the accessors, not the usage hash. GAP: cache
tokens have NO normalized accessor — they are reachable only by digging into
the raw provider-specific hash (see t/86_live_prompt_cache.t).

## Pricing source-of-truth (28b, verified 2026-08-11)

PREMISE REFUTED: there are NO per-model pricing tables in this distribution.
- `grep` over lib/Langertha/Engine/*.pm finds zero pricing constants. The one
  hit in Replicate.pm is prose in the POD ("pay-per-use pricing").
- Langertha::Pricing ships `rules => {}` (EMPTY) and `default_rule => undef`.
  All prices are CALLER-supplied per call.
- cost_for() reads only input_per_million / output_per_million; unknown model
  falls back to default_rule; missing rule yields zero cost (no die).
- UsageRecord carries a free-form `pricing_version` Str — the dist records
  which catalog a caller used but never owns one.

Consequences:
- 28e (pricing drift spot-check) is MOOT as written — nothing to drift. Close
  it or re-scope to "keep it that way".
- 28i (ADR: why pricing is not auto-fetched) is still worth writing, and is
  now really "why Langertha ships NO pricing catalog at all" — determinism,
  no network dependency, prices are the caller's business domain.
- GAP for 28f: cost_for ignores cache tokens entirely (cached input is billed
  at a different rate by Anthropic/OpenAI), and there is no per-model
  cache-rate concept. Any consumer computing real spend on a cached workload
  will be wrong. Worth its own ticket.

